Description: Vibe as a speech-to-text engine of KDE's text add-ons
 A "vibe" engine for the speech-to-text library: while the person dictates, the
 microphone is recorded at 16 kHz mono; when they stop, vibe-server (Vibe's own
 engine, unchanged) is started with "serve MODEL --host 127.0.0.1 --port 0", its ready
 line gives the port, the recording goes to its documented route
 /v1/audio/transcriptions as WAV, the text comes back to the program, and the server
 is stopped again, whatever the outcome; it also ends with its parent.
 The model is the one the person selected in Vibe (its data-home app_config.json,
 model.path) or one chosen in System Settings, Partners; the language is Vibe's own
 setting, else detected. When no model is selected, nothing is started or downloaded:
 the person is told which model Vibe's preset recommends for this computer, its size,
 and to download it in Vibe. The card is the one chosen for partners (the environment
 sparky-stereo-partners resolve gives).
 .
 Around it, in the library: an engine whose plugin metadata names a program
 (X-KTextAddons-RequiredExecutable) is not offered where that program is missing, so
 without Vibe nothing changes; an engine can say why it gave no result
 (SpeechToTextPlugin::speechToTextFailed), and the manager says why a dictation could
 not start (no engine ready, the microphone not opened, no partner for speech), so the
 action and the tool button hand that sentence to a program listening to their new
 errorOccurred signal, or show it in a message box; with no engine chosen in the
 program, System Settings, Partners, answers who takes speech
 (sparky-stereo-partners resolve --kind speech), as the text add-ons follow it for
 text, and nothing is written until the person chooses.
Author: Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack) <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-22
